"""Authored scenario/concept exercises for every remaining consolidated topic."""
def extend(qs, library):
    sources={s['id']:s for s in library['sources']}
    aliases=library['aliases']
    def add(key,ref,t,p,options,answer,e):
        ref=ref if ref.startswith('y') else 'y26-'+ref
        ref=aliases.get(ref,ref)
        qs.append(dict(id='concept-v2-'+key,type=t,category=sources[ref]['category'],sourceIds=[ref],track='concept',prompt=p,options=options,answer=answer,explanation=e))
    def S(k,r,p,opts,e):add(k,r,'single',p,opts,[0],e)
    def M(k,r,p,opts,a,e):add(k,r,'multi',p,opts,a,e)
    def P(k,r,p,opts,e):add(k,r,'match',p,opts,[],e)
    def O(k,r,p,opts,e):add(k,r,'order',p,opts,list(range(len(opts))),e)
    S('datasource','1.8','A Boot service cannot connect to its database. Which configuration should you verify first?',['JDBC driver, datasource URL, credentials, and network reachability','Only the Controller request mapping','Only the logging pattern','Whether every entity implements Runnable'],'Database access needs a suitable driver and correctly configured DataSource. Entity mappings do not fix a wrong endpoint or unavailable network.')
    S('authentication','1.17','A JWT decodes successfully but its signature does not validate. How should a protected endpoint handle it?',['Reject authentication; readable claims do not establish trust','Trust the subject because Base64 decoding worked','Allow it if its role string says ADMIN','Ignore signature checks whenever HTTPS is used'],'Decoding reveals claims; signature and required claim checks establish whether the token can be trusted.')
    S('authorization','1.18','A valid token identifies a user who lacks the required authority. What is the usual REST result?',['403 Forbidden','200 because the signature is valid','404 for every authorization failure by definition','A new administrator token'],'Authentication and authorization are distinct. A valid identity can still lack permission; 403 is the conventional forbidden response.')
    M('secret-config','1.25','Which practices help protect application secrets?',['Inject secrets through a controlled secret-management mechanism','Rotate credentials and restrict access','Keep the decryption key separate from encrypted configuration','Commit the plaintext fallback password beside an encrypted value'],[0,1,2],'Encrypting a property is insufficient if its key or plaintext fallback is exposed alongside it.')
    S('actuator','1.26','You want health and metrics endpoints for a Spring Boot service. Which facility is designed for this?',['Actuator with appropriate endpoint exposure and access controls','A public endpoint returning every environment variable','A database trigger','A Mockito spy in production'],'Actuator provides operational endpoints. Enable only needed endpoints and control access to sensitive information.')
    S('api-version','1.28','Old clients depend on a response field you want to rename. What best preserves compatibility?',['Introduce a compatible transition or version and deprecate the old contract deliberately','Rename it immediately because the server still compiles','Change GET to POST without telling clients','Require clients to inspect your database schema'],'Compatibility is defined by client contracts, not whether the provider builds. Versioning and deprecation need an explicit migration path.')
    P('profiles','1.29','Match each environment-configuration mechanism.',[['@Profile','Conditionally register a component'],['application-prod.yml','Provide profile-specific properties'],['spring.profiles.active','Select active profiles']],'Profile-specific values and conditional beans are related but different mechanisms.')
    P('breaker-states','1.30','Match circuit-breaker states to their behavior.',[['Closed','Allow calls while measuring failures'],['Open','Reject calls without contacting the protected downstream'],['Half-open','Allow limited probes to evaluate recovery']],'Half-open probing prevents sending full traffic back to a dependency before recovery has been assessed.')
    S('service-unit-test','1.34','For a focused unit test of a price-calculation service, how should you usually handle its repository?',['Inject a mock or fake and test the service behavior','Require a running production database','Start every microservice in the organization','Test only the repository SQL and skip service assertions'],'A service unit test isolates its business rules. Integration tests separately verify real persistence and wiring.')
    S('controller-test','1.35','Which test most directly checks request validation and HTTP status mapping for an MVC controller?',['A focused MVC test using MockMvc and controlled collaborators','Calling only a private helper by reflection','Checking that the database server starts','Mocking the controller method itself and asserting its stub'],'Controller tests should exercise binding, validation, routing, and response behavior rather than stub away the unit under test.')
    S('private-test','1.36','A private method contains complicated business rules. What is the preferred testing approach?',['Test observable public behavior or extract a focused collaborator','Make every method public just to expose internals','Assert only the private method name','Always use reflection as the first choice'],'Tests tied to private structure are brittle. Difficult-to-test logic may indicate a responsibility worth extracting.')
    S('void-test','1.37','A void service method sends a notification. What should its unit test assert?',['The expected collaborator call or observable state change','That the method returns true','That it returns a non-null object','Nothing, because void methods cannot be tested'],'Void means no return value, not no behavior. Verify appropriate interactions and exceptional outcomes.')
    S('fetch-plan','1.39','Changing every relationship to EAGER is proposed as a universal N+1 fix. What is the best response?',['Choose a fetch plan for each access pattern and inspect the resulting queries','EAGER guarantees exactly one SQL query','LAZY prevents any database access forever','All entity relationships must use the same fetch mode'],'Fetch timing and SQL query shape are different concerns. Fetch joins, graphs, batching, or projections may be appropriate.')
    S('central-config','1.42','Several services need consistent environment settings. What is a useful role for centralized configuration?',['Distribute versioned environment settings with controlled access and rollout','Automatically make every remote operation transactional','Remove all need for secret access control','Guarantee zero configuration mistakes without validation'],'Central delivery improves consistency, but validation, secret protection, and rollout behavior still need design.')
    S('polling','1.44','A dashboard needs updates only once a minute. Which statement is most reasonable?',['Polling may be sufficient; choose based on latency and operational needs','WebSocket is mandatory for every dashboard','Polling means the server pushes without requests','Streaming eliminates all reconnection logic'],'Avoid assuming one transport fits all update rates. Streaming improves some latency/use cases but adds lifecycle concerns.')
    S('fail-fast','1.45','A required payment endpoint is missing from configuration. What is fail-fast behavior?',['Reject startup with a clear validation error','Accept traffic and fail randomly when payments arrive','Silently replace it with an unrelated URL','Keep retrying the missing value forever'],'Fail-fast surfaces an invalid prerequisite early, where the failure is easier to diagnose.')
    S('crud-api','1.47','A successful POST creates a resource with a new identifier. Which response most clearly communicates this?',['201 Created with a Location pointing to the new resource','204 with a mandatory JSON body','500 because the identifier did not exist before','Always 200 with an exception stack trace'],'201 signals creation and Location can identify the new resource. Keep validation, persistence, and response mapping in clear layers.')
    S('jpa-owning-side','1.48','In a bidirectional Parent.children / Child.parent mapping with a foreign key on Child, which side normally owns it?',['Child.parent with @ManyToOne; Parent.children can use mappedBy','Both sides independently write unrelated foreign keys','The field with the longest name','The @OneToMany side must always own every association'],'mappedBy identifies the inverse side. Keep both in-memory sides consistent, but database relationship updates follow the owning mapping.')
    S('rest-controller','y25-115','What does @RestController combine conceptually?',['Controller handling with response-body semantics','Repository creation and database pooling','Only template-name resolution','A requirement to use XML for every response'],'RestController combines Controller and ResponseBody semantics; return values are normally written through message converters.')
    P('http-client','y25-116','Match each communication role.',[['RestTemplate','Synchronous Spring HTTP client'],['REST controller','Receives HTTP requests'],['Repository','Accesses persistence abstractions']],'RestTemplate calls remote HTTP endpoints; it is not itself a controller or a persistence repository.')
    P('body-annotations','y25-117','Match each Spring MVC annotation.',[['@RequestBody','Deserialize the HTTP request body into a parameter'],['@ResponseBody','Write a return value to the HTTP response body']],'Message converters bridge HTTP body representations and Java objects.')
    S('path-query','y25-118','For GET /orders/42?includeItems=true, which mapping is appropriate?',['@PathVariable for 42 and @RequestParam for includeItems','@RequestBody for both values without a body','@PathVariable for includeItems only','@Autowired for the order identifier'],'PathVariable binds URI-template segments; RequestParam binds query/form parameters.')
    S('spring-data','y25-119','Which statement distinguishes a Spring Data repository from the @Repository annotation?',['A Spring Data interface can receive a generated implementation; @Repository is a persistence stereotype','Annotating any interface with @Repository automatically generates every SQL query','@Repository makes all fields persistent','Spring Data repositories cannot declare derived query methods'],'Do not confuse a stereotype annotation with Spring Data repository infrastructure and its interface-based implementations.')
    P('test-levels','y25-129','Match each test scope.',[['Unit test','Exercise a focused unit with controlled collaborators'],['Integration test','Verify real components working together']],'A test that exercises actual database mapping and transaction integration is broader than an isolated service-unit test.')
    S('coverage','y25-133','A report shows 100% line coverage. What does that establish?',['The measured lines were executed, not that behavior was correctly asserted','Every possible input was tested','No defects remain','All concurrency interleavings were covered'],'Coverage tools such as JaCoCo measure execution. Strong assertions and boundary cases are still required.')
    S('openapi','y25-140','What is the relationship between OpenAPI and Swagger UI?',['OpenAPI describes an HTTP API contract; Swagger UI can render an interactive explorer','Both are database migration tools','Swagger UI automatically enforces all business authorization rules','OpenAPI requires a Java-only client'],'An API description documents operations and schemas. Runtime validation and authorization still require implementation.')
    S('thread-process','2.8','Which distinction between threads and processes is useful?',['Threads in a process share its address space while retaining their own stacks','Every thread has an entirely separate process address space','All processes automatically share every Java object','Threads require no scheduling or synchronization'],'Shared process memory enables communication but introduces data-race concerns; separate processes generally have separate address spaces.')
    S('marker','2.14','Which characteristic makes Serializable a marker interface?',['It signals a capability without declaring ordinary behavior methods','It implements every serialization algorithm as default methods','It guarantees every object graph is serializable','It forces all fields to become transient'],'The runtime serialization mechanism interprets the marker. Reachable nonserializable objects can still cause failure.')
    S('abstract-interface','2.16','You need multiple unrelated classes to share a contract, while each already extends another class. What fits Java inheritance rules?',['Have them implement an interface','Require them to extend two concrete classes','Put all instance fields in an interface','Use an abstract class and multiple class inheritance'],'Java supports multiple interface implementation but not multiple class inheritance.')
    S('diamond','2.17','A class inherits conflicting default methods from two unrelated interfaces. What should it do?',['Override the method and explicitly resolve the conflict','Rely on whichever interface appears first alphabetically','Delete all constructors','Assume the JVM chooses a default randomly'],'An explicit override resolves the conflict; allowed InterfaceName.super calls can delegate to a selected implementation.')
    S('iterators','2.18','Which iterator statement is accurate?',['Fail-fast detection is best effort and is not a concurrency-control mechanism','ConcurrentModificationException is guaranteed for every race','A weakly consistent iterator always shows a frozen snapshot','CopyOnWriteArrayList iterators see every later write'],'Fail-fast, weakly consistent, and snapshot iteration have different contracts. Do not use exceptions to implement synchronization.')
    S('jvm','2.21','Why can the same Java bytecode run on different supported operating systems?',['Each platform has a JVM implementation that executes the bytecode','The bytecode embeds every operating-system kernel','Java source code directly runs without a runtime','Every JVM uses identical machine instructions'],'The JVM abstracts platform execution; native libraries and environment assumptions can still limit portability.')
    S('reservation-design','2.24','Two requests reserve capacity concurrently. Which design best protects the available-capacity invariant?',['Enforce an atomic reservation check/update with concurrency control and a clear failure result','Check availability outside the update boundary and assume it cannot change','Store every reservation in a static unsynchronized list','Use inheritance alone to guarantee atomicity'],'Good OOP boundaries organize responsibility, but concurrent state invariants still require synchronization or transactional/versioned updates.')
    S('pass-by-value','y25-007','A method appends to a StringBuilder parameter, then assigns that parameter a new builder. What does the caller observe?',['The append on its original object, but not reassignment of its variable','Its variable points to the new builder automatically','Neither effect, because object mutation is impossible','Java sometimes passes references by reference based on object size'],'Java copies the reference value. Both copies can refer to the same mutable object, but parameter reassignment changes only the local copy.')
    P('strings','y25-011','Match each Java text type.',[['String','Immutable character sequence'],['StringBuilder','Mutable builder without synchronized methods'],['StringBuffer','Mutable buffer with synchronized methods']],'StringBuffer synchronization does not automatically make a multi-call business operation atomic.')
    S('access','y25-012','A member has no explicit access modifier. What is its ordinary accessibility?',['Within its package','From every class in every package','Only by subclasses in any package','Only by the declaring object instance'],'The default member access level is package-private. Protected additionally supports subclass access subject to Java access rules.')
    S('hashset','y25-014','How does HashSet decide that an added element duplicates an existing one?',['Using hashCode to locate candidates and equals to compare them','Using only toString output','Using insertion position alone','Requiring object identity for every element'],'HashSet uses hash-based membership backed by a map. Stable, compatible equals/hashCode behavior is essential.')
    P('final-family','y25-022','Match these similarly named Java features.',[['final','Restricts reassignment, overriding, or inheritance depending on context'],['finally','A cleanup block in exception-handling control flow'],['finalize','Legacy finalization hook that must not be relied on for cleanup']],'Prefer explicit resource management or try-with-resources. Abrupt process termination can prevent finally from running.')
    S('thread-start','y25-023','What is the difference between t.start() and directly calling t.run()?',['start schedules a new thread of execution; run is an ordinary call on the current thread','run always starts two new threads','start and run are always interchangeable','start can be called repeatedly after the thread terminates'],'A Thread instance can be started only once. Directly invoking run does not create a new thread.')
    P('runnable-callable','y25-024','Match task contracts.',[['Runnable.run','Returns void and cannot declare checked exceptions'],['Callable.call','Returns a value and may throw an Exception']],'ExecutorService can submit these tasks. Future retrieval exposes results or execution failures.')
    S('object-monitor','y25-025','Why are wait and notify associated with Object monitors?',['Coordination is tied to the lock protecting shared state, not one specific Thread instance','Every Object owns a permanently running thread','They can safely be invoked without holding the relevant monitor','notify transfers arbitrary object references between processes'],'Monitor ownership and a shared condition define the coordination protocol. A waiting thread must recheck its condition after waking.')
    S('join','y25-026','The current thread calls worker.join(). What is the intended effect?',['Wait for worker to terminate, subject to interruption and any timeout overload','Merge both thread stacks into one','Start worker if it has not been started','Acquire every lock held by worker'],'join is a completion-waiting mechanism, not a thread-start or lock-transfer operation.')
    M('thread-states','y25-027','Which are actual Java Thread.State values?',['RUNNABLE','TIMED_WAITING','TERMINATED','RUNNING'],[0,1,2],'Java does not expose RUNNING as a separate enum state. RUNNABLE includes execution and eligibility to execute.')
    S('sleep-wait','y25-028','A thread owns an object monitor. How do sleep and wait differ regarding that monitor?',['wait releases that monitor while waiting; sleep does not release it','sleep releases every lock; wait releases none','Both automatically release every lock held by the thread','Neither can be interrupted'],'wait requires ownership of the relevant monitor and releases that monitor while waiting; it does not release unrelated locks.')
    S('latch','y25-029','Three workers must finish before a coordinator continues. Which CountDownLatch use is appropriate?',['Initialize with 3; each worker counts down; the coordinator awaits','Initialize with 0 and expect workers to increase it automatically','Reset the same latch to 3 after each batch using reset()','Call await on every worker before allowing any countdown'],'CountDownLatch is one-shot. Once its count reaches zero, subsequent awaits pass; it has no reset operation.')
    S('semaphore','y25-030','acquire() is interrupted before obtaining a permit. Should a finally block unconditionally release one?',['No; that can add a permit that was never acquired','Yes; acquire always succeeds before throwing','Yes; release verifies permit ownership and will do nothing','Only if the semaphore count was initially zero'],'Semaphore does not enforce ownership. Structure cleanup so each successful acquisition has one matching release.')
    S('read-write-lock','y25-035','What does a conventional read/write lock allow?',['Concurrent readers when no writer owns the write lock; exclusive writing','Unlimited writers as long as no reader is waiting','A write operation under the read lock is always safe','Every reader must execute one at a time'],'Read/write separation can help read-heavy workloads, but workload, fairness, and critical-section duration affect the result.')
    S('transient','y25-037','A transient instance field has value 42 before default Java serialization. What is true?',['Its value is excluded from default serialization unless custom logic handles it','Its value is automatically encrypted','It becomes static on deserialization','Every serialization library must ignore it regardless of configuration'],'transient applies to default native serialization behavior; it is not encryption and does not impose rules on all other formats.')
    M('java-eight','y25-043','Which features were introduced with Java 8?',['Lambda expressions','Stream API','Default interface methods','The Java Platform Module System'],[0,1,2],'Java 8 introduced lambdas, streams, and default methods. The module system arrived in Java 9.')
    P('pattern-selection','y25-055','Match a design problem to a suitable pattern.',[['Interchangeable algorithms behind one contract','Strategy'],['Constructing objects behind a creation abstraction','Factory'],['Notifying interested listeners about changes','Observer']],'Patterns communicate recurring design intent; choose one based on the actual collaboration problem.')
    S('abstract-factory','y25-061','A UI toolkit needs matching Windows buttons and menus or matching Mac buttons and menus. Which pattern fits?',['Abstract Factory for compatible product families','Singleton solely to prevent all object creation','Observer solely to sort the products','A global lock instead of a creation abstraction'],'Abstract Factory creates related families, while a simpler factory commonly abstracts creation of one product type.')
    S('builder','y25-064','A configuration object has many optional fields and must be valid when published. What is a useful Builder role?',['Collect construction choices and validate before producing the completed object','Mutate every previously built instance whenever a new field is set','Automatically make mutable fields deeply immutable','Guarantee no validation is ever needed'],'Builder separates construction steps from the final representation. Immutability and validation still require deliberate implementation.')
    S('observer','y25-068','A price update should notify several subscribed displays. What is the Observer relationship?',['The subject notifies registered observers of a change','Every display must inherit the subject implementation','The subject creates exactly one global display','Observers replace the subject database transaction manager'],'Observer decouples the event source from interested listeners. Manage subscription lifetimes to avoid unintended retention.')
    S('chain','y25-069','A request passes through authentication, validation, and processing handlers. Which pattern describes this structure?',['Chain of Responsibility','Abstract Factory','Singleton by definition','Object pooling'],'Handlers can process or forward a request according to a chain policy. The policy defines whether processing stops or continues.')
    S('sql-nosql','3.1','A team proposes a database solely because it is labeled NoSQL. What should the review focus on?',['Actual access patterns, transactions, consistency, and operational trade-offs','The assumption that every NoSQL database has identical semantics','Whether the name contains SQL','The assumption that SQL databases cannot scale horizontally'],'Database families are broad labels. Evaluate concrete capabilities against application requirements.')
    S('indexes','3.4','Why not create an index on every column?',['Indexes consume space and add write-maintenance costs; usefulness depends on queries','Indexes always reduce every write cost','Every database allows only one index total','Indexes replace all uniqueness and foreign-key constraints'],'Use execution plans and workload measurements to select useful access paths.')
    P('cqrs','3.8','Match these data-access concepts.',[['Query','Retrieve information without an intended business-state change'],['Command','Request a business-state change'],['CQRS','Separate command and query responsibilities/models']],'CQRS does not automatically require separate physical databases or eventual consistency in every implementation.')
    S('eventual-consistency','3.9','A replica returns an older value briefly after a write, then converges. Which model does this illustrate?',['Eventual consistency','A guarantee that every read sees the latest completed write','No durability by definition','A Java checked exception'],'Eventual consistency allows temporary divergence; the application must decide whether stale reads fit the use case.')
    S('salary-ties','3.11','You must return all employees at the third-highest distinct salary. Which approach preserves ties?',['Filter DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) = 3 in an outer query','ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2 always returns all ties','Use COUNT(*) = 3 without ranking salaries','Select only the third inserted employee'],'Dense ranking numbers distinct salary levels without gaps. A single offset row does not represent every employee at that salary.')
    S('cdc','3.12','What does change data capture provide?',['A stream of committed database changes for downstream processing','A guarantee that every replica is always immediately current','A replacement for every database transaction','A method to prevent all schema changes'],'CDC transports changes, often from database logs. Consumers still need schema, ordering, and duplicate-handling policies.')
    S('sql-injection','y25-070','Which query construction prevents user-supplied values from becoming SQL syntax?',['Use a PreparedStatement with bound values','Concatenate input after removing spaces','Surround arbitrary input with quotes by hand','Base64-encode the input then concatenate it'],'Parameter binding separates values from query structure. Dynamic identifiers need validation or an allowlist.')
    S('jdbc-template','y25-073','What does JdbcTemplate reduce?',['JDBC resource-management boilerplate and inconsistent SQLException handling','The need to choose correct SQL','The need to define transaction boundaries','Every database round trip automatically'],'JdbcTemplate handles common JDBC mechanics and exception translation, while application query and transaction design remain necessary.')
    O('jdbc-transaction','y25-074','Order the success path of a local JDBC transaction using a fresh connection.',['Disable auto-commit','Execute the related SQL statements','Commit the transaction','Close or return the connection'],'On failure, roll back as appropriate and still close/return the connection. Connection state must be suitable for reuse in a pool.')
    S('orm','y25-075','Which statement about ORM is accurate?',['It reduces mapping boilerplate but does not eliminate SQL and fetch-plan tuning','It guarantees every query is faster than JDBC','It makes schema design irrelevant','It prevents all N+1 queries automatically'],'ORM is a mapping and persistence abstraction, not a universal query optimizer.')
    S('hibernate-thread-safety','y25-077','Does obtaining a Hibernate Session through getCurrentSession make it safe for concurrent use by multiple threads?',['No; current-session context management does not make Session thread-safe','Yes; the word current implies a global synchronized Session','Yes, but only for writes','Only if the SessionFactory has exactly one entity mapping'],'Share the SessionFactory, not one mutable persistence context across concurrent work.')
    P('entity-states','y25-079','Match each persistence state.',[['New/transient','Not yet made persistent in a context'],['Managed','Associated with the current persistence context'],['Detached','No longer associated with that context'],['Removed','Marked for deletion']],'Dirty checking applies to managed state. Detached changes do not automatically persist through the old context.')
    S('get-reference','y25-080','Why is a lazy entity reference not a guaranteed existence check?',['Database access and a missing-entity failure may be deferred until initialization','Every reference always inserts the entity first','A proxy guarantees a row exists forever','Reference creation must perform every possible join'],'A provider may supply a proxy without loading the entity immediately; choose lookup APIs according to the required semantics.')
    S('merge-return','y25-081','After managed = entityManager.merge(detached), which object should you use for managed-state updates?',['The returned managed instance','The detached argument, which must become managed','Either object because they must be the same reference','Neither, because merge always deletes the entity'],'merge copies state into a managed instance. The original detached argument does not become managed just because merge was called.')
    P('flush-clear-commit','y25-082','Match persistence operations.',[['flush','Synchronize pending context changes with the database'],['clear','Detach all entities from the persistence context'],['commit','Complete the transaction successfully']],'Flush is not commit: SQL may execute while the transaction can still roll back. Clear does not persist pending changes by itself.')
    S('criteria-types','y25-087','Which Criteria approach provides stronger compile-time checking of entity attributes?',['Use a generated static metamodel instead of arbitrary string attribute names','Assume root.get("typo") can never fail','Concatenate all user input into HQL','Avoid specifying a result type in every query'],'A programmatic query API can still contain runtime string-name errors. Typed metamodel attributes improve compile-time checking.')
    O('hibernate-cache','y25-088','For an entity-by-ID lookup eligible for caching, order the usual fallback checks.',['Check the current persistence context','Check the enabled second-level cache','Load from the database on a cache miss'],'The first-level context is local to the Session; the second-level cache can share eligible entity data across contexts.')
    S('cte','y25-093','What is a common table expression primarily useful for?',['Naming a query result within a statement to structure a complex query','Permanently creating a new physical table in every database','Guaranteeing every query runs faster','Removing the need for joins'],'WITH defines a query-scoped expression. Materialization and optimization behavior depend on the database and query.')
    P('procedure-trigger','y25-094','Match these database mechanisms.',[['Stored procedure','A callable routine that can accept parameters'],['Trigger','Logic invoked by a configured database event']],'A trigger runs in response to its event rather than an ordinary explicit procedure call. Both need careful transaction and side-effect design.')
    S('window-rank','y25-096','For descending salaries 100, 90, 90, 80, what are RANK and DENSE_RANK respectively?',['RANK: 1,2,2,4; DENSE_RANK: 1,2,2,3','RANK: 1,2,3,4; DENSE_RANK: 1,2,3,4','RANK: 1,2,2,3; DENSE_RANK: 1,2,2,4','Both collapse the two employees earning 90 into one row'],'Both preserve tied rows. RANK leaves a gap after ties; DENSE_RANK numbers distinct ordered values without gaps.')
    S('monolith','4.1','A small team does not need independent service deployment. Which assessment is reasonable?',['A modular monolith may reduce operational complexity while preserving boundaries','Microservices always reduce debugging complexity','A monolith cannot have modules','Every module requires a separate database server'],'Service distribution introduces network, consistency, and operational costs. Choose boundaries and deployment granularity deliberately.')
    S('async-communication','4.2','A user submits a long-running export. Which design can avoid holding the HTTP connection for the full job?',['Accept the job and expose status or completion notification','Keep retrying the same synchronous request without an identifier','Return success before storing or accepting any work','Remove error handling because the job is asynchronous'],'Asynchronous acceptance separates request latency from job execution, but still needs durable acceptance, status, and failure handling.')
    S('performance-measure','4.5','A service is slow and the team proposes more instances immediately. What should guide the decision?',['Measure the bottleneck and test whether scaling that component improves the limiting resource','Assume every bottleneck is CPU','Increase all retry counts first','Remove every timeout'],'Scaling the wrong component can increase pressure on a shared database or dependency. Measure before optimizing.')
    S('kafka-streams','4.6','What is Kafka Streams?',['A client library for processing Kafka data, including stateful transformations','A replacement name for every Kafka broker','A Java collection with no relationship to Kafka','The mandatory metadata server for every Kafka cluster'],'Kafka Streams builds processing applications on Kafka; it is not a broker or metadata coordinator.')
    P('kafka-components','4.10','Match Kafka components.',[['Producer','Publishes records'],['Broker','Stores and serves partition data'],['Consumer','Reads records'],['Topic partition','An ordered log within a topic']],'Partitioning provides parallelism; a topic can contain multiple independent ordered partition logs.')
    S('kafka-rabbit','4.12','Which distinction is useful when choosing between Kafka and RabbitMQ?',['Kafka centers on retained partition logs and replay; RabbitMQ commonly centers on routed queue delivery','Kafka cannot retain consumed records','RabbitMQ has no exchange routing','Both guarantee one global order across every consumer'],'Delivery, retention, replay, ordering, and workload requirements matter more than treating either system as universally superior.')
    S('partition-key','4.13','Events for one order need partition-local ordering. What producer choice supports that goal?',['Use a stable order identifier as the record key with a compatible partitioning scheme','Use a new random key for every event of that order','Require every consumer to sort unrelated topics globally','Assume keyless records always go to one permanent partition'],'Stable keys typically colocate related records. Partition-count or partitioner changes can affect the mapping.')
    S('broker','4.14','What is a Kafka broker?',['A server that stores partitions and handles produce/fetch requests','A single business message','The integer position of a record','A Java method that commits every database transaction'],'A broker participates in the cluster and serves partition data; brokers and consumer groups are distinct concepts.')
    S('isr','4.15','Why distinguish the replication factor from ISR?',['Replication factor is the configured copy count; ISR tracks sufficiently caught-up replicas','They always refer to identical counts under all failures','ISR is the number of consumer groups','Replication factor is the number of records per batch'],'A configured replica may be out of sync. Acknowledgment and minimum-ISR settings affect availability and durability behavior.')
    S('consumer-failure','4.16','A consumer disappears from a group. What normally allows progress to continue?',['Group membership recovery and reassignment of its partitions','Deleting the entire topic','All consumers sharing one in-memory offset variable','Changing every producer key to null'],'After failure detection and rebalance, another consumer can resume from committed offsets, potentially replaying uncommitted work.')
    S('retention','4.17','Does consuming a Kafka record automatically delete it from the log?',['No; retention or compaction policies govern removal','Yes; the first consumer permanently removes it','Only if the consumer uses Java','Yes; committed offsets physically overwrite every record'],'Consumption position and log retention are separate. Retained data can be replayed subject to policy and available offsets.')
    S('offset-reset','4.19','When is auto.offset.reset relevant to a Kafka consumer?',['When no usable committed offset exists for the assigned partition','On every successful poll regardless of committed offsets','Whenever any message contains a null value','Only when the producer closes'],'It specifies where to begin when a valid committed position is unavailable; it does not override every existing committed offset.')
    S('virtual-memory','4.20','What does virtual memory provide?',['An address-space abstraction mapped onto physical memory and backing storage','Unlimited physical RAM without cost','A guarantee that the OS cannot terminate a process under pressure','The same concept as Java object serialization'],'Address-space abstraction does not remove physical resource limits. Paging and memory pressure can affect performance.')
    P('throughput-latency','4.21','Match these performance measurements.',[['Throughput','Completed work per unit of time'],['Latency','Time taken for one operation'],['p99 latency','A percentile describing the slower tail of observations']],'High throughput and low tail latency are different goals; batching can improve one while worsening the other.')
    P('cache-writes','4.22','Match cache access patterns.',[['Cache-aside','Application loads the backing store on a cache miss'],['Write-through','Write updates the cache and backing store synchronously through the pattern'],['Write-behind','Backing-store writes are deferred after cache acceptance']],'Deferred writes introduce durability and ordering concerns; cache-aside needs an invalidation/update strategy.')
    P('ttl','4.24','Match expiration approaches.',[['Lazy expiration','Check whether an entry expired when it is accessed'],['Active expiration','Background work removes expired entries']],'Systems can combine these approaches. Logical expiry and physical memory reclamation need not occur at the same instant.')
    S('read-optimization','4.26','A list endpoint reads many rows and skips increasingly large offsets. What may improve stable sequential paging?',['Cursor/keyset pagination using a suitable ordered key','Returning the entire table on every page','Increasing page size without limit','Adding an unrelated index without inspecting the query'],'Keyset pagination can avoid repeatedly scanning skipped rows. Cursor semantics must match a deterministic ordering.')
    S('write-optimization','4.27','How can batching help a write-heavy workload?',['Amortize per-request overhead while controlling batch size and latency','Guarantee that larger batches always reduce tail latency','Eliminate the need for transaction boundaries','Make non-idempotent retries safe automatically'],'Batch size trades throughput against latency, memory use, and failure recovery. Measure and bound batches.')
    S('gateway','y25-065','Which responsibility is commonly placed at an API gateway?',['Edge routing and selected cross-cutting access policies','Owning every service business table','Making service-level authorization unnecessary','Replacing all internal domain logic'],'A gateway centralizes some edge concerns, but services still own domain rules and must enforce suitable authorization.')
    P('rabbit-routing','y25-149','Match RabbitMQ exchange types.',[['Direct','Exact routing-key matching'],['Topic','Pattern-based routing-key matching'],['Fanout','Broadcast to bound queues'],['Headers','Header-attribute matching']],'A direct exchange may route one message to multiple queues with the same matching binding key.')
    S('cicd','5.1','A pipeline builds a JAR and pushes a Docker image to a registry. Has it necessarily deployed the application?',['No; the target runtime still needs a deployment/update step','Yes; registries execute every image as a service','Yes; a successful unit test creates a Kubernetes Pod','Only if the JAR filename contains prod'],'Artifact production and deployment are distinct stages. A registry stores images; a runtime platform runs them.')
    S('ec2','5.3','Changing an EC2 instance to a larger CPU/memory type is what kind of scaling?',['Vertical scaling','Horizontal scale-out','Kafka partition rebalancing','Database normalization'],'Vertical scaling changes one instance capacity; horizontal scaling changes how many instances serve the workload.')
    S('s3','5.7','Which workload is a natural fit for S3?',['Storing objects such as images, exports, and backups by key','Running an arbitrary always-on JVM process inside a bucket','Providing a normal in-process Java HashMap','Replacing all relational foreign-key constraints'],'S3 is object storage. Compute execution and relational transaction semantics are different services/capabilities.')
    S('lambda','5.8','What is a useful description of AWS Lambda?',['Event-driven managed function execution','An EC2 instance that you must always keep running manually','A Java lambda expression with no deployment environment','A database index type'],'Serverless functions move infrastructure management to the platform, but startup behavior, limits, and dependency access still matter.')
    S('registry','5.9','What does a service registry help clients discover?',['Available service instances and their locations','Every user password','The entire source code of each service','A universal global database transaction'],'Discovery maps logical services to reachable instances; health and lifecycle updates keep that mapping useful.')
    S('load-balancing','5.10','An instance is running but not ready to serve requests. How should readiness affect routing?',['Exclude it from serving traffic until ready','Route more traffic to force it to become ready','Treat process existence as sufficient readiness','Delete every healthy instance'],'Readiness is about traffic eligibility. Liveness and startup checks answer different lifecycle questions.')
    M('scaling-metrics','5.11','Which signals can inform autoscaling when appropriately measured and configured?',['CPU utilization','Queue depth per worker','Request rate or latency indicators','Only the number of source-code files'],[0,1,2],'Choose metrics connected to load and capacity; thresholds, stabilization, and dependency bottlenecks need consideration.')
    P('network-protocols','5.14','Match these protocol concepts.',[['TCP','Reliable ordered byte-stream transport'],['UDP','Datagram transport without built-in delivery guarantees'],['HTTP','Application-layer request/response semantics']],'These operate at different abstraction levels. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP, so HTTP must not be universally equated with TCP.')
    S('public-private','5.15','In a digital-signature scheme, which key normally creates the signature and which verifies it?',['The private key signs; the corresponding public key verifies','The public key signs; only the private key can verify','Both keys must be published to every client','No key is needed if Base64 encoding succeeds'],'Signing and encryption are distinct operations. Keep the signing private key secret and distribute trusted verification information.')
    S('tls','5.16','What does a TLS certificate primarily help establish during HTTPS connection setup?',['A binding between an identity and a public key, subject to certificate validation','That the application has no authorization bugs','That every HTTP response is semantically correct','That all data at rest is encrypted'],'TLS protects the communication channel when correctly validated; application-level correctness and permissions remain separate.')
    S('ecs-eks-flow','5.17','Which flow describes a common external request path into a containerized service?',['Load balancer routes to healthy service targets, which handle the request','An image registry executes the HTTP request directly','A Terraform state file returns the business response','An autoscaler substitutes for the service code'],'Load balancing, compute scheduling, discovery, and scaling cooperate but have different responsibilities.')
    P('helm-kubectl','5.19','Match Kubernetes tooling.',[['kubectl','Interact with Kubernetes resources through the API'],['Helm chart','Package parameterized Kubernetes manifests'],['values.yaml','Supply chart configuration values']],'Helm helps package and manage releases; kubectl remains a general cluster API client.')
    S('vpc','5.20','What is the role of a VPC?',['Provide a logically isolated virtual network with configured subnets and routing','Automatically make every subnet reachable from the internet','Store every application password as plaintext','Replace all application authorization checks'],'Public reachability depends on routes, gateways, addressing, and security controls, not just membership in a VPC.')
    S('vpn','5.21','What is a common VPN purpose?',['Create an encrypted tunnel between networks or a client and a network','Turn every internal database into a public website','Guarantee every tunneled application request is authorized','Replace database backups'],'A VPN protects connectivity across a tunnel. It does not remove the need for application authentication and authorization.')
    O('architecture-flow','y25-146','Order this stated synchronous request flow through an application.',['Client sends an HTTP request','Gateway routes it to the selected service','Service validates business rules and accesses persistence','Service returns a response through the gateway','Client renders the result'],'This is one explicit architecture flow. Gateways and persistence are not mandatory for every possible request.')
    S('cloud-comparison','y25-157','In an interview, you used Azure but are asked about AWS. What is an accurate approach?',['Explain the AWS concept and identify a comparable capability without claiming direct usage','Claim production AWS experience because all clouds are identical','Avoid discussing the actual project environment','Assert that service names are interchangeable with identical behavior'],'Separate general knowledge from firsthand experience, and explain where comparisons are conceptual rather than identical.')
    S('cloudwatch','6.4','What does a CloudWatch alarm do?',['Evaluate configured metric conditions and trigger configured state-change actions','Automatically fix every application defect','Replace all log instrumentation','Guarantee that an alarm threshold is appropriate without tuning'],'Alarm rules need meaningful thresholds and responses. Metric evaluation and remediation are separate concerns.')
    S('heap-dump','6.6','What information is a heap dump especially useful for?',['Inspecting objects and reference-retention paths at a point in time','Listing every future request the service will receive','Proving a leak from total heap size alone','Replacing all CPU and thread diagnostics'],'Heap analysis helps find retained objects and roots. Combine it with workload and GC trends; collection can have operational cost.')
    S('distributed-debug','y25-147','A request intermittently fails across several services. What investigation best connects the evidence?',['Follow a correlation/trace identifier through logs, metrics, dependencies, and recent changes','Restart every service before collecting any evidence','Assume every failure is a JVM memory leak','Inspect only the frontend button color'],'Build a timeline of the request and its dependencies. Diagnose impact and cause before choosing a targeted remedy.')
    # Deeper exercises for the preserved Stream reference and integrated OOP content.
    S('stream-lazy','y25-extra-stream','A stream pipeline contains filter and map but no terminal operation. What normally happens?',['The element-processing pipeline is not traversed yet','All source elements are immediately transformed','The source collection is always overwritten','A new thread is created for every element'],'Intermediate operations describe a lazy computation; a terminal operation drives traversal.')
    P('stream-functions','y25-extra-stream','Match Stream operations to the relevant functional interface.',[['map','Function'],['filter','Predicate'],['forEach','Consumer'],['sorted with a custom ordering','Comparator']],'Track both element types and the role of each supplied function.')
    S('stream-flatmap','y25-extra-stream','You have a Stream<List<String>> and need one Stream<String>. What operation fits?',['flatMap(List::stream)','map(List::stream), which already returns Stream<String>','count()','sorted() alone'],'map creates one output element per input, here a stream; flatMap flattens the nested streams.')
    S('stream-type-order','y25-extra-stream','After map(Person::getName), why is sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Person::getAge)) invalid?',['The stream now contains String elements, not Person objects','Streams cannot be sorted','map always ends the pipeline','Comparators can only compare primitive arrays'],'Either sort the Person stream before mapping or use a comparator suitable for the mapped element type.')
    M('stream-empty-match','y25-extra-stream','For an empty stream, which statements are true?',['allMatch(predicate) returns true','noneMatch(predicate) returns true','anyMatch(predicate) returns false','findFirst() returns a nonempty Optional'],[0,1,2],'Universal conditions over an empty stream are vacuously true; no element exists to satisfy anyMatch or findFirst.')
    S('stream-reduce-empty','y25-extra-stream','What does Stream.<Integer>empty().reduce(0, Integer::sum) return?',['0','Optional.empty()','null','A NoSuchElementException'],'This overload has an identity and returns a value. The no-identity overload returns Optional.')
    S('stream-reduce-associative','y25-extra-stream','Why is subtraction a poor reduction operator for a parallel sum-like reduction?',['It is not associative, so grouping partial results can change the result','All integer operators are forbidden in streams','Parallel streams require string results','Subtraction always throws an exception'],'Parallel reduction may regroup work. A valid reduction needs an associative operator and a compatible identity.')
    S('stream-reuse','y25-extra-stream','After calling count() on a Stream, can you safely call collect() on the same stream instance?',['No; obtain a new stream from the source','Yes; every terminal operation resets it','Only if count returned zero','Only if the source is an ArrayList'],'A stream is intended for one pipeline traversal. Reuse after consumption is not supported.')
    S('stream-order','y25-extra-stream','Which terminal operation preserves encounter order when an ordered parallel stream emits side effects?',['forEachOrdered','forEach guarantees order on every parallel stream','findAny guarantees the first element','unordered always preserves order'],'forEachOrdered honors encounter order if present. Ordering and safe shared mutation are separate requirements.')
    S('stream-collect','y25-extra-stream','Why prefer collect(toList()) over adding to one shared ArrayList inside parallel forEach?',['The collector coordinates accumulation and combination without that unsafe shared mutation','An ordinary ArrayList becomes synchronized inside a lambda','forEach always executes on one thread','Collectors disable all parallel processing'],'Use stream reduction mechanisms rather than unsafely mutating a shared non-thread-safe container.')
    S('stream-array','y25-extra-stream','Which operation creates an Integer[] from Stream<Integer>?',['toArray(Integer[]::new)','toArray(String[]::new)','count()','reduce(Integer::sum)'],'The array generator supplies the intended runtime array type. The no-argument overload returns Object[].')
    P('stream-terminals','y25-extra-stream','Match terminal operations to their result shapes.',[['count','long'],['min with comparator','Optional of an element'],['forEach','void'],['collect(groupingBy(...))','Map of grouped results']],'An empty stream affects optional and aggregate results differently; choose the overload and collector deliberately.')
    S('stream-findany','y25-extra-stream','Which statement about findAny is valid?',['It may return any element, even when the source has encounter order','It must always return the last element','It guarantees the first element in every parallel run','It returns the element count'],'Use findFirst when the first element of an encounter-ordered stream is semantically required.')
    S('stream-window','y25-extra-stream','What is Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5).skip(2).limit(2).toList()?',['[3, 4]','[1, 2]','[2, 3]','[4, 5]'],'skip removes the first two elements; limit retains the next two. This example uses the Java 16+ Stream.toList method.')
    S('stream-parallel','y25-extra-stream','When is parallelStream guaranteed to be faster than stream?',['There is no universal guarantee; measure the actual workload','Whenever there are at least two elements','Whenever the source is an ArrayList','Whenever the lambda performs blocking I/O'],'Parallelism adds splitting, scheduling, combining, and contention costs. Source structure and operation cost matter.')
    S('oop-composition','2.6','A Car delegates starting to an Engine field. Which relationship does this model?',['Has-a composition/collaboration','Car must be a subtype of Engine','Multiple class inheritance','Method overloading by itself'],'Composition expresses collaboration through a contained reference; inheritance expresses a substitutable is-a relationship.')
    S('oop-dispatch','2.6','Animal a = new Dog(); a.sound(); calls an overridden Dog.sound(). Which principle is illustrated?',['Runtime polymorphism','Method selection based only on the variable name','Compile-time overloading only','Serialization'],'For an overridden instance method, runtime dispatch selects behavior using the actual object type.')
    S('oop-encapsulation','2.6','Why keep a mutable collection field private and avoid returning its live reference?',['To protect invariants from uncontrolled external mutation','To make every operation automatically thread-safe','To eliminate all object allocations','To prevent the class from having any public methods'],'Encapsulation controls access to state. Thread safety and defensive-copy costs are separate design decisions.')
    S('rabbit-direct-many','y25-149','Two queues are bound to one direct exchange with binding key order.created. A message has that routing key. What happens?',['Both matching queues can receive a copy','Only one queue may ever match a direct exchange','The message is necessarily discarded','It must be converted into a fanout exchange first'],'Direct means exact binding-key matching, not a one-queue limit.')
    S('rabbit-dlx','y25-149','Which distinction between a DLX and a dead-letter queue is correct?',['The DLX is an exchange that routes eligible dead-lettered messages to bound queues','The DLX is always the consumer thread itself','Every unroutable initial publish automatically becomes a dead letter','A dead-letter queue cannot have an exchange binding'],'Dead lettering originates from eligible queue events/policies. The exchange and the destination queue have different roles.')
